register(StarFlagT);
define
    sfSrc="ORIG";
    sfBag;
    sfClean;
    sfRun code={
        sfBag +% sfSrc;
        sfFace <- sfBag["sfSrc"];
        cerr "SF-0 the two bodies -- if these DIFFER a flag must not cross:":;
        addrOf(sfSrc);
        addrOf(sfFace);
        sfSrc :. noPrinT;
        sfPlain  =  sfFace.noPrinT;
        sfStar   = *sfFace.noPrinT;
        sfCtlP   =  sfClean.noPrinT;
        sfCtlS   = *sfClean.noPrinT;
        sfSrcP   =  sfSrc.noPrinT;
        sfSrcS   = *sfSrc.noPrinT;
        cerr "SF-1 face   NO star   = " sfPlain:;
        cerr "SF-2 face   WITH star = " sfStar:;
        cerr "SF-3 MISS control never written  NO star   = " sfCtlP:;
        cerr "SF-4 MISS control never written  WITH star = " sfCtlS:;
        cerr "SF-5 HIT control the SOURCE      NO star   = " sfSrcP:;
        cerr "SF-6 HIT control the SOURCE      WITH star = " sfSrcS:;
        };
    ;
sfRun();
print "STARFLAG SENTINEL -- reached the foot":;
stop();

starFlagT -- `*x.flagName` IS NOT A FLAG READ, AND THIS IS THE ACCEPTANCE FIXTURE FOR
THE CHAINED-DOT CHANGE. Minted 2026-09-15 on Tony's ruling. Its rows are what FLIP when
`.` becomes a binary left-associative operator; until then they pin what the language
does today, which is not what anyone reading `*x.flag` expects.

WHY IT EXISTS. incant/fixits/faceFlagsNoCross spelt its forward row `*fcFace.noPrinT`
and read 1; incant/pop/faceT asks the identical question as `faFace.noPrinT` and reads
0. One character, opposite answers, and the citizen was one commit from retiring as
"fixed" on the strength of it.

⚠⚠ THE ROW THAT SETTLES IT IS SF-6, AND IT IS WHY THE CONTROLS ARE ARRANGED THIS WAY.
SF-1 against SF-2 is only a disagreement -- it says one of them is wrong and not which.
SF-5 and SF-6 ask the SAME question of the SOURCE FIELD THE FLAG WAS JUST WRITTEN TO,
where the answer is not in doubt. The plain read says 1. The starred read returns the
local's own TAG -- no data at all. A spelling that cannot read a flag off the field that
carries it is not reading flags anywhere, so SF-2's 1 is noise rather than an answer.

    read                 NO star        WITH star
    the face             0              1
    never written        0              tag echo
    the SOURCE           1              tag echo

⚠ THE TAG ECHOES ARE PINNED BY VALUE, DELIBERATELY, and this is not a bear-trap #26
re-offence. Pinning an echo as if it were data is the trap; pinning it as the EVIDENCE
THAT THERE IS NO DATA is the assertion, and it is presence-with-value rather than
absence-of-message (rule H4) -- the row prints a value on every run and compares it.
The moment the starred read starts returning a number, these two rows go red and
somebody re-reads. That is the whole job.

⚠ SF-0 IS LOAD-BEARING. If the two bodies were the SAME body, SF-1's 0 would be a defect
rather than the correct answer, and the fixture would be measuring something else. `+%`
attaches a COPY and `<-` MINTS a copy (a ruled defect, Tony 2026-08-23, not the design),
so the face is a copy of a copy and the bodies differ. SF-1 reading 0 is the language
being right.

MECHANISM, and it is already recorded rather than new: bear-trap #48's second half. `.`
is a member of the UnaryOPS bin as well as being registered opDot, so `*a.b` is TWO terms
and KANT-43's right-to-left association applies the star LAST -- `*(a.b)` -- a star
applied to a flag's VALUE. Banked as bear-trap #26 payment seven, which cross-references
this file.

WHAT FLIPS WHEN THE CHAINED-DOT ITEM LANDS -- A PRE-REGISTERED PREDICTION, GRADED AS A
PREDICTION AND NOT AS A FACT. If `.` becomes binary and left-associative, `*a.b` folds
as `(*a).b` instead, and the star then binds the NAME rather than the flag's value:

    SF-2  1        -> 0     the face, dereferenced, still does not share the body
    SF-4  tag echo -> 0     the control reads an unset flag honestly
    SF-6  tag echo -> 1     THE ROW TO WATCH: the source reads its own set flag

    SF-1, SF-3, SF-5 must NOT move. They contain no star and the change is about
    association, so a move there means the fold reached something it should not have.

GRADE: mechanism-grounded, which in this codebase is the half that usually holds -- the
association rule is read directly off the grammar and the registration, not inferred from
a symptom. It is still a prediction.

⚠⚠ AND THE THIRD OUTCOME, NAMED BECAUSE THIS FIXTURE IS EXACTLY THE CASE THAT NEEDS IT:
THE TREATMENT CAN VOID THE CONTROL. The chained-dot change touches this fixture's own
read machinery, so a run where SF-5 or SF-1 moves does not mean the prediction failed --
it means the discriminators stopped discriminating and every row below them is
UNINTERPRETABLE rather than wrong. Report that as a voided control. Do not grade it.
(Ratified 2026-08-10 on the KANT-8 bracket rung, which failed in precisely this way.)

⚠ THE POSTFIX FOLD IS BLOCKED TODAY AND THE BLOCKER IS NOT REPETITION -- designDocs
PostfixFoldBlocked carries the four-spelling table and the two controls that moved the
blame: a one-term ALIAS between TokenXP and InvokeArg kills the language as thoroughly as
a `*` does, so InvokeArg must be referenced by TokenXP directly. Every flag-class modifier
lives on that slot and every repetition-class one dies. That is the wall this fixture is
waiting on.
